all. This minimizes the risk of others completing melds while preserving opportunities to form higher-value patterns like Pung of F3 (for Prevalent Wind + Dragon Pung) or J2 (Dragon Pung).  \nReason: Discarding T9 avoids exposing a tile that could be used in Chows or Pungs by opponents. Keeping F3 and J2 in hand supports potential high-point melds, while T9\u2019s terminal nature reduces immediate risk.  \nAnswer: Play T9"}
